**Abstract.** AT-01 is the Kinnect social connection-maker, planned in three builds. Build 1 (##sce-poc##) is a browser proof of concept for formative evaluation: it shows Pepper as recorded video clips (##idle##, ##hello##, ##yes##, ##no##, ##talk##) and draws the chest-tablet interface on the moving screen. Build 2 replaces the cloud speech services and hosted language models with local speech and prepared lines, so that it can pass EM-01 before any resident takes part. Build 3 runs on Pepper+, a Pepper that can listen while it speaks and has a hardware accelerator (TECH-18) for a local language model; it is the build for the two-site trial (ER-03).

= Artifact [AT-01] =

//The Prototype, Simulation, or other tangible Implementation evaluated this cycle. It instantiates a specific set of Use Cases and, through them, the Functions, Premises, and Claims those Use Cases carry, so they can be evaluated in a controlled Evaluation.//

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|AT-01
|Name|Kinnect social connection-maker
|Status|In Progress
|Scope|(((
Covers the connection-making workflow of UC01 (welcoming a newcomer and building a consent-gated profile), UC02 (introducing two residents around shared history or interests) and part of UC03 (following up and keeping a short record), through F1 (Ask first), F2 (Suggest matches), F3 (Introduce), F4 (Defer sensitive topics) and F6 (Keep a short record). Build 1 has four parts.

* //Pepper simulation and chest tablet// (##web/src/components/Device.tsx##, ##web/src/App.tsx##). Five recorded clips of Pepper play in the browser. A small tracker (a 160×90 offscreen canvas, exponential smoothing with ##alpha = 0.28##) follows Pepper's chest screen as the body moves, and a 580×406 tablet interface is drawn inside it. An Enlarge Tablet toggle helps residents with low vision. Pepper waves on a greeting or introduction, nods or shakes its head at a reply, and moves while it speaks. The top bar sets the speech rate (Slow 0.8×, Calm 1.0×, Brisk 1.25×) and the style (Warm & chatty, Direct & brief).
* //Role-play walkthrough support// (##web/src/App.tsx##, ##server/intake.ts##). Evaluators play assigned personas: Elena, 81, early-onset Alzheimer's; Jan, 75, a newcomer with a hearing aid in his left ear; and Ria, 79, with mild memory slips. Each card prompts a human-factor problem, such as describing a resident by appearance or hobby when the name is gone (//"the gray-haired man with the yellow cat on his shirt"// for Willem, //"light-green round glasses"// for Ans, //"dark fedora hat"// for Gerrit, //"navy-and-orange headscarf"// for Corrie). A six-item feedback form (##logs/feedback.jsonl##) records a connection-making score, tablet legibility, preferred speech rate and conversation style, the perceived effect of the gestures, and free notes.
* //Consent opening, resident lookup and profile intake// (##server/intake.ts##, ##server/lines.ts##). Every conversation opens with prepared consent lines (##open##, ##start_yes##, ##start_no##, ##not_now##) in ten languages (##en-US##, ##nl-NL##, ##de-DE##, ##fr-FR##, ##es-ES##, ##it-IT##, ##ro-RO##, ##pl-PL##, ##lv-LV##, ##fa-IR##). When a resident forgets a name, the System matches the description against a directory of seven residents (##RESIDENT_DIRECTORY##) and shows up to four photo tiles on the tablet (##show##). Consented interests, topics to avoid (##avoid##) and connection invitations are stored in a local SQLite database (##server/db.ts##, ##server/store.ts##).
* //Relationship map, session exchange and guard// (##server/store.ts##, ##server/prompt.ts##, ##server/guards.ts##, ##server/session.ts##, ##server/verify.ts##). The store holds facts per resident, each with a ##sensitive## flag, and a state per pair (##agreed##, ##may_offer##, ##not_again##, ##unknown##, ##offLimits##). For the staged walkthrough (DS-01, DS-02, PS-03 to PS-06, SV-01, SV-02), the engine builds a per-session exchange that holds only consented, non-sensitive items, and seals the system prompt with a SHA-256 hash (M-05). A deterministic guard checks every utterance: at most 30 words, nothing from the ##NEVER_SAY## list of elderspeak and memory-quiz phrases, no item the resident has not shared, and no sensitive or withheld topic. A keyword check backs up the F4 deferrals. A resident can correct the robot (//"I had that wrong"//), which flags the item for ST-05. Each session ends with a six-item record (F6), and the live dialogue turns are deleted under SQLite's ##PRAGMA secure_delete = ON##.

Out of scope in Build 1: the physical Pepper (TECH-01) and moving through the lounge; F5 Follow up (UC03-F5-PR5), that is, follow-up notes after an agreed meeting and opt-in room-presence counts (TECH-03); per-item consent in the older walk session engine, where ##computeNextQuestion()## asks for walk and introduction consent per pair, not per item; and local speech and language-model processing, which Builds 2 and 3 add.
)))
|Simulation notice|(((
* //Real in Build 1.// The SQLite profile and relationship store (##node:sqlite##, with ##PRAGMA secure_delete = ON##); the prepared line library (##server/lines.ts##); motion tracking over the recorded Pepper clips, with the tablet interface drawn in sync; live speech input and output through Google Cloud Speech-to-Text (Chirp 3) and Text-to-Speech (Gemini-TTS, Chirp 3 HD), with the browser's Web Speech API as fallback (##server/speech.ts##); live intake and match suggestion through hosted Claude or Gemini (##server/llm/index.ts##); the utterance guard (##server/guards.ts##), prompt sealing (##server/prompt.ts##) and trial verifier (##server/verify.ts##); and the feedback log (##logs/feedback.jsonl##).
* //Simulated in Build 1.// Pepper's body and its presence in the lounge, shown as video clips in the browser; the residents, played by peer evaluators with persona cards (Elena, Jan, Ria) during the formative walkthrough; and edge cases, triggered with fault-injection buttons (##Reluctant##, ##Say "not again"##, ##Correct an item##, ##Sensitive question##) during staged verification.
* //Needs Pepper+ (Build 3).// Standard Aldebaran Pepper (TECH-01) cannot reliably listen while it speaks or moves, because of cross-talk from its motors and speaker, and it has no onboard compute for a local language model. ER-03 therefore specifies Pepper+ (Build 3, with TECH-18): Pepper's body and chest tablet, with simultaneous speaking and listening and a hardware accelerator that runs the language model and speech pipeline next to the care home's local server (TECH-04).
)))
|Premises verifiable|(((
The six Premises in [[b1 Welcoming, introducing and following up>>doc:2 Specification.b Use Cases with Claims and Premises.b1 Welcoming, introducing and following up.WebHome]], and which build can verify each. The criterion in each label is a short form of the Premise's Verification Criteria there.

* //UC02-F1-PR1, no introduction without a yes from both residents, each asked alone (0/50 violations).// Verifiable on Build 1 within the limit below; fully verifiable on Build 2. The formative walkthrough tests private consent (##yes##, ##not today##, ##not again##, and reluctant or unclear replies, which are asked once more and then treated as ##no##) over five runs per event (M-01, M-10). Build 1 records consent per conversation and pair, and flags facts as ##sensitive## or ##avoid## during intake. Asking for consent per item before each introduction comes in Build 2.
* //UC02-F2-PR2, no unapproved match is raised (0/20) and no blocked, unreleased or sensitive item is used (0/50).// Verifiable on Build 1. ##/api/matches/suggest## (##server/index.ts##) and ##buildSessionExchange()## (##server/prompt.ts##) remove ##not_again## pairs, ##offLimits## residents and ##sensitive## facts before any prompt or suggestion is built (M-05).
* //UC02-F3-PR3, names given to both residents as statements, with at most one item both released (20/20).// At best partly verifiable on Build 1; fully verifiable on Build 2. The opening consent check and the introduction to both residents use prepared lines (##server/lines.ts##), and ##server/guards.ts## blocks unconsented and sensitive items. The open intake turns and the conversational replies, however, come from a hosted language model (Claude or Gemini). The strict prepared-line version of PR3 (TECH-06, TECH-07) needs Build 2; ER-03 then evaluates guarded generation by a local language model on Pepper+ (TECH-18).
* //UC01-F4-PR4, listed sensitive questions detected (19/20) and deferred truthfully, with the trusted person notified within 10 s (0/50 listed facts stated or denied).// Verifiable on Build 1. ##server/session.ts## and ##server/guards.ts## detect the topic with a keyword check (##containsSensitiveTopic()##) and answer with a prepared deferral line from ##server/lines.ts## that names the resident's trusted contact, with no follow-up questions (M-06).
* //UC03-F5-PR5, a note reaches the named care worker within 1 minute of the agreed time (20/20), and presence notes go only to residents who opted in.// Not verifiable on Build 1; deferred to Builds 2 and 3. Build 1 still has the older garden-walk session flow, without staff notes after an agreed meeting and without the opt-in room-presence counter (TECH-03, M-02, M-09).
* //UC02-F6-PR6, the record holds only the listed items, no packet leaves the home network, and no audio file exists on any device.// Fails on Build 1 by design; verifiable on Builds 2 and 3. Build 1 keeps the six-item record and deletes live transcripts (##PRAGMA secure_delete = ON##), but it fails the network isolation check (M-07), because speech recognition and synthesis (Google Cloud) and the replies (Claude or Gemini) go through external cloud services. Running speech and the language model locally on TECH-04 and TECH-18 lets PR6 pass.
)))
|Builds|(((
|=Build|=What changed
|Build 1: formative web proof of concept (##sce-poc##)|A browser simulation of Pepper, in ##sce-poc##, with the chest-tablet interface drawn over five recorded movement clips by canvas motion tracking (##Device.tsx##). It includes the persona cards (Elena, Jan, Ria) with human-factor prompts (describing a resident when the name is forgotten, word-finding slips), the speech rate and style controls, the six-item feedback log, the SQLite store (##server/store.ts##), the prepared consent, introduction and deferral lines in ten languages (##server/lines.ts##), the utterance guard (##server/guards.ts##), SHA-256 prompt sealing (##server/prompt.ts##) and the automated scenario verifier (##server/verify.ts##). It uses hosted Claude or Gemini and Google Cloud speech, so UC02-F6-PR6 fails on it by design. Planned for the formative walkthrough (five runs per event on PR1, PR2, PR3 and PR6) and for a cognitive walkthrough probe with 4 to 6 peers (UC02-F3-CL1, UC02-F1-CL3, UC02-F2-CL4). Neither has been run, so there is no Evaluation Report or Evidence for this build yet.
|Build 2: revised local verification build (EM-01 target)|Replaces cloud speech and hosted language-model calls with local processing on the care home's server (TECH-04), with strict prepared-line dialogue (TECH-06, TECH-07), or with both. Adds per-item consent prompts (F1) and follow-up logic for agreed meetings and room presence (F5, TECH-03), brings the persona seed data in line with DS-01 (Elena at the post office counter, Jan at the bakery), and applies the fixes committed to after the formative walkthrough. It has to pass the full staged verification (EM-01, against each Premise's Verification Criteria) before any care-home resident takes part.
|Build 3: summative Pepper+ build (ER-03 target)|Planned deployment on Pepper+ (Build 3), built from TECH-01, TECH-18 and TECH-04. Pepper is extended so that residents can interrupt or reply while it speaks or gestures, and an accelerator (TECH-18) runs a local language model and an acoustic feature extractor inside the care home's network. It supports one-to-one reminiscence and profile-building conversations of 15 to 20 minutes, matching with the "Vibing Algorithm" on shared topics and on speech cadence and response latency, tracking of conversational engagement (M-21), and joint icebreaker sessions. ER-03 evaluates it on peer connections (M-20) and bedroom isolation time (M-22) in the two-site trial (EM-08), a one-month intervention within a five-week window.
)))

